I'm sure I'll again get comments that my views are "revolting," but
let me state some more realities:

* much of knowledge is the art of bullshitting. Or, to mix metaphors,
like walking on a high wire without a net: you'd better be confident
(dynamic optimism?) or you'll surely fall.

* "quick-studies," a trait seen in many of the folks on this list,
need to be able to quickly, on-the-fly, and on-their-feet absorb the
basics of a new area and then use past experience to apply these
basics in new ways.

* careful, painstaking study has its place in science, usually in
expertimental sciences like chemistry and biology, but is usually not
suited for speculative leaps.

* I think this is another gender difference. Many women I know in the
sciences are a bit like Amara--they want to work things out completely
before presenting results. Unfortunately, in most areas of business,
argument, persuasion, and even research, this approach moves too
slowly. People have moved on to other things, the staff meeting has
already finished talking about an area, and so on.

(This ability to "bullshit"--which is much different than lying, and
in fact is just a term for a kind of self-confident speculation--is
what girls in school need to be taught. Otherwise, they get
steamrollered in technical areas.)

And the connection with the "debating style" I mentioned in my earlier
post is of course clear: in debate, one argues with confidence
regardless of one's underlyning believes. "Switch-side debates" are
especially illustrative of this. (Contrary to what someone, perhaps
Arthur Hlavaty, said about no longer trusting my statements, being
