All I'm arguing is: "Vivre la difference!," to coin a phrase. 

Political systems which attempt to defy certain basic human
characteristics and force-fit women inot a mold they are perhaps not
suited for is crazy. The Procrustean bed of "equal opportunity" is but
one example.

This is not to say that women should be excluded. (As Mike L. points
out, "should" is itself a dangerous word.)

In a free employment market, women who are good astrophysicists may
find jobs (probably not many, for other reasons). If they're not, they
won't. And in a free economy, a potential employer will take into
account a person's marriage status, fertility status (based on visible
indications, presumably), and will try to gauge how likely it is that
she'll stay on the job.

A "fact of life" is that women pretty much need to bear their children
before they're 40-42, and the odds of a birth defect rise dramatically
after about 36-38. Amara's hopes for egg-freezing are still basic
research....who knows when it'll come?

Waiting until age 40-45 to thaw out the eggs and have a kid is perhaps
an even more disruptive career move, as that is when most successful
people are either involved in starting their own companies or are in
line for V.P-type positions. I won't pursue this point now.

I think women are in fact more interested in inter-personal
relationships, in children, and in the concrete aspects of living. I
think men are more interested in external things, in "the hunt," in
sex for simple gratification, and in abstract concepts.

I'm not arguing for discrimination by law, nor for unequal educations.
In a free society, people would hire who they want to hire and
educate their children as they see fit.

What I'm arguing is that the whole modern agenda of feminism, equal
rights, and so on, is missing some important points about gender
differences. Ironically, if this is true, then it is the women who
will suffer the most.

