I think the real reason is that there probably aren't very many artists
on the list,  and/or that many of the people on the list are overspecialized
as technical/professional types.  Or,  that the artistic types on the list
don't speak up.  Well, here goes.

Robert Anton Wilson has a couple of neat little articles in The Illuminati
Papers.  One of them is directed toward artists,  the other toward
scientists, both asking the question: are you only using half of your
brain?

I for one,  being a musician,  would welcome discussions of how extropian
ideas can be applied to the arts.  I'll start with my own ideas on applying
the principle of spontaneous order to my own work,  and how it relates to
the improvement of my perception in a manner which is quite deliberate.

The principle of spontaneous order as applied to music is something I
personally find very interesting.  My approach to this is to write music
in a collaborative, improvisational manner.  When a group of musicians
are attuned to each other creatively,  there is a kind of "group
personality" that arises,  that is not under the control of any one
member.  Thus the group is participating in the creation of a spontaeous
order.  

How is this cultivated?  The most important skill is being able to divide
one's attention,  so that there are multiple layers of perception operating
simultaneously.  This is necessary in order to perceive both the individual's 
contribution and the spontaneous order itself.

Here are the ways I use to develop this skill:

1. The Mind at Rest

In order to be able to divide one's attention with precision,  the ability
to focus and limit attention must be cultivated.  This is done with
traditional meditation techniques to be able to bring the mind to a state
of rest, the Zen master's "empty mind".

2. Rhythmic Exercises

One way to develop the division of attention is to perform musical
exercises that require the division of attention.  A simple example is
to tap out four beats with your left hand on a table, and tap out five
in the time of these four with your right hand.  Endless variations of
this can be devised.  Another, more difficult, way to do this is to
develop a facility to improvise multiple distinct melodic lines at
once.

3. Conversations

Another way to develop the division of attention is to,  in social
situations,  practice following multiple conversation threads at once.
There is no need to actually participate in the conversations,  just
to be aware of where each of them are.  Eventually you develop an
awareness of where people are without them necessarily taking part
in a conversation either.  This is particularly applicable to group
improvisation because it is in a sense musical conversation,  and
there can be multiple simultaneous musical conversations.

4. Listening

One of the most important ways to develop the division of attention
is to listen to music that is complex enough that there are multiple
musical threads (counterpoint, in musical terminology),  and attempt
to follow the threads together.  There is music that is so complex
that it requires this kind of listening to understand it,  for example
some of the works of Elliott Carter or Roger Sessions.  One of the
most exhilarating feelings as a listener is to be able to perceive
what is really happening in a piece of music that was at first not
intelligible at all,  but became so through the improvement of one's
ability to perceive.  This improvement in perception,  once attained,
is available to take to the next level of complexity.

Does the division or layering of attention show up in other people's 
interactions with spontaneous orders of other kinds?  I've found that
this occurs in some team sports in a similar manner to a musical
group,  and is probably just as necessary to participate in spontaneous
orders in that arena (e.g. triple plays).

Andy

