Date: Tue, 7 Dec 93 14:59:33 PST
From: Robert Brooks <rb@hprrb.rose.hp.com>
Subject: Vinge, The Singularity, and Despair

Jay,

Just wanted to let you know that I valued your message, and hope you
stay on.  Any movement needs its critics.  And your message certainly
wasn't "debating the basics", in my opinion: it poses some good questions
that, to my knowledge, haven't been adequately addressed.

Perhaps I could offer some encouragement:

1.  I like what little I've seen of your writing (just that here on the list);
    I'm sure you will achieve some success--keep trying.  Some ideas:
      a.  Put together an on-line anthology and offer it for sale on the net.
          I'll pledge 5 bucks for it.  (Yeah, you'll get flamed for
          "commercial activity on the Internet".  Who cares.)
      b.  Get into multimedia, somehow.  Maybe you could be an author/editor/
          consultant for a company in the new media arena, whose current
          product is CD-ROM "games" for PC's.  The focus will move on-line
          within 5 years, and will likely exceed the print media market
          within 10.

2.  Who knows what barriers there are to Jupiter-brainness.  Maybe
    consciousness isn't scalable.  Maybe a significantly accelerated
    consciousness would inevitably die of boredom.

3.  I'm not sure wealth is a necessary prerequisite to uploading.  The
    first uploads will likely be volunteer research subjects.

4.  We don't even begin to understand consciousness yet, regardless of
    Vinge's or Minsky's claims, so it's ridiculous to put a date on the
    Singularity.  It could be hundreds of years.

5.  Don't worry about the identity issue.  If each change is incremental,
    no more perceptible than the effect of drinking a cup of coffee, it's
    hard to see how identity could be discontinuous.

6.  There is little reason to suppose that human minds or their environment
    will be assimilated or consumed by superbrains.  After all, a 
    biological "gray goo" hasn't taken over the planet yet.  Such beings
    would likely find a more energy-rich environment more attractive for
    tapping (like the sun), and would likely find humans more valuable
    un-interfered with, for entertainment if nothing else.

    There is no reason to think that life as an ordinary human (or as
    anything lower than the leading edge superbrains, for that matter)
    would not be worthwhile under such a scenario.  After all, most have
    lived under a supposition of the existence of gods for millenia.

    In fact, this argument for the existence of gods, that if such a thing
    is possible then it has likely already happened in some prior evolution,
    and our universe is in fact the creation of some race of superbeings,
    seems rather compelling.  But it only points to deism, not theism, and
    I don't see how deism differs from atheism, operationally.


Robert

